L\\sim\\eta(2/\\theta_{\\rm j}^{2})L_{\\rm BZ}\\sim 10^{2}L_{\\rm BZ}. 

Using Equations (1) and (5), the strength of the magnetic field is determined by the observed luminosity L as

B_{\\rm H} \\sim 3\\times 10^{12}\\ \\left(\\frac{\\eta/\\theta_{\\rm j}^{2}}{10^{2}} \\right)^{-1/2}\\left(\\frac{M_{\\rm BH}}{3M_{\\odot}}\\right)^{-1}  \\times\\left(\\frac{L}{10^{47}{\\rm erg\\ s}^{-1}}\\right)^{1/2}\\ \\ { \\rm G}.

A characteristic timescale of the BZ jet activity is determined by the pressure balance near the BH. As the mass accretion rate decreases, the pressure of the fallback matter p_{\\rm f} falls short of the magnetic pressure p_{\\rm B} (making a magnetically-arrested disk; e.g., Bisnovatyi-Kogan & Ruzmaikin, 1976; Narayan, Igumenshchev & Abramowicz, 2003). Then, the magnetic flux expands the torus and decreases on the BH, leading to the reduction of the BZ power. The pressure of the magnetic field is

p_{\\rm B}=\\frac{B_{\\rm H}^{2}}{8\\pi}\\left(\\frac{R}{r_{\\rm H}}\\right)^{-4},  under the magnetic flux conservation, where R is the radial distance. The pressure of the fallback matter is

p_{\\rm f}=\\frac{GM_{\\rm BH}\\dot{M}}{2\\pi R^{3}v_{\\rm R}},  where v_{R} is the radial velocity. For the radial velocity v_{\\rm R}, we assume v_{\\rm R}\\equiv\\epsilon v_{\\rm ff} where v_{\\rm ff}=\\sqrt{GM_{\\rm BH}/R} is the free-fall velocity. For the value of \\epsilon, we adopt \\epsilon\\sim 10^{-2} which is supported by the observations and numerical simulations of the relativistic jets (e.g., Tchekhovskoy, Narayan & McKinney, 2011; Zamaninasab et al., 2014). Then, the magnetospheric radius R_{\\rm m}, where the equilibrium point between two pressures p_{\\rm f} (Equation 7) and p_{\\rm B} (Equation 8), is

\\frac{R_{\\rm m}}{r_{\\rm H}} \\sim 2\\ \\left(\\frac{\\epsilon}{10^{-2}}\\right)^{2/3}\\left(\\frac{B_{ \\rm H}}{10^{12}{\\rm G}}\\right)^{4/3}  \\times\\left(\\frac{M_{\\rm BH}}{3M_{\\odot}}\\right)^{4/3}\\left( \\frac{\\dot{M}}{10^{-11}M_{\\odot}\\ {\\rm s}^{-1}}\\right)^{-2/3}.

Using Equation (9) and the temporal evolution of the mass accretion rate (Equation 4), we obtain the characteristic timescale of the BZ jet

T \\sim 1\\times 10^{4}\\ \\left(\\frac{\\epsilon}{10^{-2}}\\right)^{-3/5}\\left( \\frac{t_{\\rm vis}}{0.1{\\rm s}}\\right)^{2/5}\\left(\\frac{M_{\\rm f}}{10^{-3}M_{ \\odot}}\\right)^{3/5}  \\times\\left(\\frac{M_{\\rm BH}}{3M_{\\odot}}\\right)^{-6/5}\\left(\\frac {B_{\\rm H}}{10^{12}{\\rm G}}\\right)^{-6/5}\\ {\\rm s}.

Using Equations (6) and (10), the total fallback mass is determined by the observed luminosity L and duration T as

M_{\\rm f} \\sim 1\\times 10^{-2}M_{\\odot}\\ \\left(\\frac{\\epsilon}{10^{-2}}\\right) \\left(\\frac{\\eta/\\theta_{\\rm j}^{2}}{10^{2}}\\right)^{-1}\\left(\\frac{t_{\\rm vis}} {0.1{\\rm s}}\\right)^{-2/3}  \\times\\left(\\frac{L}{10^{47}{\\rm erg\\ s}^{-1}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{ T}{10^{4}{\\rm s}}\\right)^{5/3}.

After the time t>T, the BZ power evolves as L_{\\rm BZ}\\propto t^{-40/9} derived by the magnetic flux \\Psi_{\\rm BH}\\propto R_{\\rm m}^{-2} and the time dependence R_{\\rm m}\\propto t^{10/9} from Equations (4) and (9). Thus, we model the BZ power as

L_{\\rm BZ}\\propto\\left(1+\\frac{t}{T}\\right)^{-40/9}. 

The maximum value of the BZ power L_{\\rm BZ} is determined by the accretion power Mc^{2}. In fact, using the condition p_{\\rm f}>p_{\\rm B} and Equations (1), (7) and (8), the ratio is L_{\\rm BZ}/(\\dot{M}c^{2})\\lesssim 1. For comparison, we plot the mass accretion rate with the beaming correction (2/\\theta_{\\rm j}^{2})\\dot{M}c^{2}\\sim 10^{3}\\dot{M}c^{2} in Figures 2 and 3 as thin dotted lines.

Our theoretical light curve is consistent with the observations. As an example, we plot the observational data7 of short GRB 070714B, which has extended and plateau emission in Figure 2.

